import{_ as i}from"./ComicFlash.vue_vue_type_script_setup_true_lang-mJfD_zwu.js";import{e as n,o as r,c as h,d as a,w as o,b as l,J as u,u as s,S as d,a3 as c,a as e,an as m,F as g}from"./index-Bd5ug0Z9.js";import{C as p}from"./ControllerModes.enum-DFzYijiO.js";import"./MobileController.vue_vue_type_script_setup_true_lang-RDmZkGHC.js";const T=n({__name:"004692HS",setup(y){return(f,t)=>(r(),h(g,null,[a(u,null,{default:o(()=>[...t[0]||(t[0]=[l("[S] Past Karkat: Wake up.",-1)])]),_:1}),a(i,{story:s(d).Homestuck,mobileControls:s(p).Normal,secretsActive:"","flash-file":"act-5-act-2/02791.swf",alt:`A flash game has you play as Karkat, Terezi, or Vriska in the hideout, exploring and interacting with the others.
    Karkat wakes up and gets the lowdown from Terezi: they've been in contact with humans from the universe they created, who may or may not be paradoxically related to their circumstances and the key to their escape! Karkat is pretty much completely uninterested, and talks about his horrible dream where he died. Terezi knows! They all died! Karkat has some guesses as to who the demon is, but needs to do a bit more searching before he has a clear answer. He immediately begins going around and telling everyone to ignore the humans and stay awake no matter what. He also goes out and explores the lab, coming across the room he has so many horrible memories in: the ectobiology room. Slime and wigglers, everywhere. Being covered in babies. It was awful. He was pretty much freaking the fuck out.
    Back in the main room, each of the trolls react a bit differently, depending on who you are.
    Sollux has a bit of an attitude toward everyone. He prods at Karkat for being squeamish around blood, essentially tells Vriska to fuck off, and judges Terezi for her obsession with the 'insufferable prick'. Terezi counters by saying that she thinks Dave and Sollux are cool in similar ways.
    Feferi gets a bit concerned for the bad dream Karkat seemed to have, but just tries to troll Vriska. She seems adamant that they can't hide here forever, and Terezi questions what they should do if a thousand Aradiabots couldn't kill Noir. She has an answer: the humans!
    Aradiabot agrees that the humans are obviously the solution, but says that they are also the problem. 'Enjoy engineering the present conundrum as you try to solve it,' she says. Aradiabot just gets kind of snippy with Vriska and Karkat and threatens both of them. She implies Karkat's stupid angry impulses and incoming decision will paradoxically cause every problem they have or ever will have, and will lead to the great undoing itself. She thinks about killing Karkat to prevent their deaths in an offshoot timeline, but decides she doesn't want to kill her friends and that their fate is sealed anyway. Everyone kind of sees Aradiabot as an unsettling tin psychopath. Far in the future, she explodes.
    Tavros is getting adjusted to his new robo-legs. Karkat calls him an idiot for not paying any attention to the demon that killed them all, and tells him to spread the word that it blew up Prospit. Tavros falls over. Vriska pressures him into walking up and down staircases for 'practice', saying it's what Rufio would want him to do. Terezi says to never let anyone tell him Rufio isn't real, and that believing hard enough in imaginary things makes them less fake. Wise words, Terezi! Tavros is immediately off to go practice walking up and down the stairs, despite Equius' warnings.
    Equius is pretty much being his normal weirdo self. Karkat orders him to never do any robo-leg horseshit ever again, to which Equius eagerly obliges. Karkat orders him to stop getting off on his orders. Equius starts sweating like crazy and says he needs to go towel off. Karkat says he should just get Aurthour to do it, immediately realizing Aurthour's death is a sore subject and apologizes. Equius suggests a sweaty, reconciliatory bro hug, to which Karkat replies: 'Don't touch me.' Vriska teases Equius about all of this.
    Nepeta tries to roleplay with Vriska, but immediately shuts it down after Vriska tries to assume the roles of her characters that do really mean things. Nepeta's favorite human, Jade, thinks that her attempts at friendship are just teasing, but Nepeta is determined to purrservere. Karkat orders her to cut the roleplay bullshit with the humans entirely. He immediately flips on this and tells her to be as obnoxious as possible after she says Equius already gave her the same order.
    Kanaya is obviously infatuated with Rose. She finds her 'sort of intoxicating'. Intoxicatingly underwhelming, to be exact. In the future, Kanaya asks Karkat about the Green Sun, but can only get the info that it's somehow related to Noir. Kanaya intensely brushes off any conversation Vriska tries to have, and has been ignoring her for a long time now.
    Gamzee is just kind of guarding a giant pile of horns he's left on the floor. He makes a generous offer to Karkat: 420 boondollars for a nap on the pile. For some strange reason, Karkat refuses and tells him to 'clean his fucking garbage off of the floor'. Gamzee makes a similar offer to Terezi, who politely declines and says she might jump onto the pile some time later. This scares him a little bit... the noise would be startling! Gamzee doesn't make the offer to Vriska at all. He's scared of her. Vriska thinks this is kind of cute.
    Eridan is kind of an emotional wreck. He tries to get Terezi to deliver a message to Sollux about meeting for a duel. Terezi immediately calls him on his ashen solicitation, and says Sollux would be uninterested in any kind of rivalry. Eridan starts chewing his cape and realizing how pathetic he is. Much later, after powering through an awkward conversation with Vriska, Rose blows up Eridan's computer and he gets really mad. Vriska teases him about sensing some kind of black romance. When Karkat talks to him, he just shits on Gamzee for his stupid horn pile and pats himself on the back for 'having the courtesy to hide his wand pile somewhere else'. That's great, man. Really a step above that guy, you are.
    Vriska tells Karkat that she has made a bunch of plans for the humans while he was asleep. She's got sooo many irons in the fire... Karkat frustratedly asks her to stop, or at least put her plans on pause. Despite initially not wanting to bother with the humans at all, her plans make him nervous and he wants to look into things first. He promises to make some kind of speech if she stops and asks nicely, using the magic word: 'please' with eight E's. Vriska agrees and takes a break. She begins exploring the lab and finds things similar to the machinery in the SkaiaNet laboratory: meteor-tracking machines, a dodecascreen, and busted lab tubes. She also runs into a massive two-headed chess piece monster with udders. Out of sheer luck, the monster falls off of a cliff. Beyond Vriska's stash of boondollars intended to give John a helping hand (and now rendered useless by Terezi's massive donation from Mr. Strider), there's a passcode-locked room. Vriska eventually fills the room with Nick Cage posters and a marble Nick Cage bust... it's a whole shrine to the guy. Upon returning to talk to Terezi, she says she'd feel ashamed if she got rich via some secret admirer instead of gathering the treasure herself. Just blatant jealousy on display. Vriska keeps prodding her about her old, embarrassing flarp name, and clearly misses their 'friendly rivalry' as the Scourge Sisters. Vriska then starts talking about competition about who would be better at manipulating a chumpy, impressionable boy. Terezi is not having it, and questions the 'friendly' part of their rivalry entirely.
    Terezi does a bit more exploring than the rest. Initially, she gets disappointed about never getting to hang out with Karkat on Prospit. She had so many plans! It was supposed to be special! Karkat seems to not really give that much of a shit or see what the big deal is, which just frustrates her and makes her give up on the conversation to go walk around. After making her way through the easiest fucking sliding puzzle of all time and a giant mess of stairs, she comes across the telescope (sorry, smelloscope) on the rooftop and smells the exploded remains of Prospit. She later smells the remains of Derse and puts on an awesome dragon suit for some roleplay. After returning past her Strider-esque chalk wall murals, she finds Nepeta, and they begin the scene.
    The mighty and astounding dragonyyyd legislacerator enters the esteemed pouncellor's office with an urgent but friendly requisition pursed magically in her majestic snout. The distinguished pouncellor nods in the most dignified and legal of manners at the dashing and well dressed legislacerator. She double checks a series of impurrtant legal clawses and rubber stamps them with wild abandon for the most judicial sort of approval pawsible. This all looks to be in order! (She says.) Please follow me! (She also says.) Nepeta takes her to a secret room with her drawing tablet and Eridan's shitty pile of wands. At least someone has the decency to keep his trash tucked away. Nepeta warns against napping in the wand pile, and says everyone is banned from doing so. Disobeying and sleeping in the wand pile leads to an instant Game Over.`},null,8,["story","mobileControls"]),a(c,{"next-page-link":"/004693","link-text":"John: Connect to Jade."}),a(m,{pageRange:"004692"},{default:o(()=>[...t[1]||(t[1]=[e("p",null,"As the command implies, you wake up as Karkat after a long, grim nap. He was asleep when Prospit was destroyed, killing his dream self, and trapping him in his nap for a while, for mysterious reasons. Also, regardless of the narrative purpose it’s serving, or questions it will answer: this was an EXCITING HOMESTUCK MOMENT, dropping this page out of nowhere. It was another one of those moments where readers had no idea this was a format in Homestuck’s arsenal, then it drops without any warning at all, and with barely any pause preceding it (maybe a few days in this case). And just like that, this type of content becomes “something Homestuck is capable of doing, and may continue doing in the future”. And that did turn out to be true. There were at least another three games like this. One later in this book where John explores a village, and the aforementioned one with Rose and John, and another one involving Equius, Nepeta, and Gamzee during the throes of murderstuck. Each had different tonalities and story purposes. The Rose/John involved a melancholy atmosphere, and a sense of dramatic irony over slain parents on a roof. The latter was essentially pure horror genre. But this one here, the atmosphere is mainly, HEY LOOK! THIS IS A THING WE CAN DO NOW! IT’S PRETTY COOL! By my recollection, there were few who disagreed.",-1),e("p",null,"Immediate dividends of this format included the introduction of the “talk sprite”. People loved these things, made their own, and looked forward to any additional representations of the characters that looked like this. They added some flavor to the dialogue, showing facial expressions to go along with certain lines, which I think definitely changed the feel of the dialogue compared to the previously unillustrated pesterlogs. Since this was a new idea, and also since these games got made with such INSANE speed, by a variety of artists without the luxury of style sheets or anything, they came across as a little wonky in this first game. All over the place in terms of style. But that’s Homestuck for you. They served the purpose, and were all pretty good on their own terms.",-1),e("p",null,"Obviously you can talk to anyone you want first when you gain control of Karkat. Here in the book it sorta makes sense to check with Feferi first, since it gets right to the point of “what happens when you go to sleep without a dream self”. Karkat ominously advises against this. Feferi is a good foil for this topic, because it reminds us that in an earlier memo we saw, three hours after Karkat wakes up, Derse is destroyed too, which traps Feferi in her nap like Karkat just was. In fact, she knows this already, because she got the memo too and wants to trap herself in the nap deliberately. We’ll keep tabs on that. Another key topic of this game is, Karkat waking up to everyone buzzing about these new alien kids they found. So this gives us insight into Karkat’s first exposure to them, and the origin to his decision to organize a trolling effort against them. It’s kind of an interesting wrinkle to the troll timeline. It shows the rest of the trolls were pretty deep into interacting with the humans before Karkat even knew about them, which is not the impression we got when we first started hearing from Karkat. He’s actually pretty late to the party, which shouldn’t surprise us by now.",-1),e("p",null,"The horn pile is established through this game as a critical fixture in the troll lab, referenced repeatedly, and almost always in the context of being used as a bed. It exists, aside from the reason that Gamzee is a sloppy useless clowny piece of complete garbage, for literally no other reason. He dances by it perpetually throughout this game, also for no reason. Another thing this game can do, which Homestuck in general can also do, is spontaneously hop to another point in the timeline to check something out. In this case, “Be Future Feferi” hops three hours ahead to when she’s sleeping on the horn pile, trapped in her Dark Nap. And we quickly return from that future vignette to resume play. This is one way we flesh out the full series of timeline events.",-1),e("p",null,"Time to catch up with Terezi now. And due to the expanded nature of this format, on this page we make it... a full SIX lines deep into this conversation before we have to turn the page. Perhaps it’s time to address the elephant in the room. This game (again, a single page on the website), occupies about ONE HUNDRED AND FIFTY PAGES in this book. Was there a better way to do this, to save space? Probably. Maybe we could have made like, maps? Compressed the conversations into a linear, non-illustrated text format, by omitting some of the talksprites? Maybe we could have cut the page count in half or such. Would that have been worth it? We WOULD lose a few things, the flavor to the conversations that the talk sprites add and such. Also, the more you mess around with trickery like that, the more the layout decisions start piling up. And pretty soon you realize, it’s just easier to start charging through this material with pure screencaps alone, and maybe at the end of the day, that’s fine, and it’s a decent way to read all this in a book, and nobody’s actually complaining, except me. And even I’m not. I’m just typing long ass posts down here as usual, which for some unfathomable reason I have taken to calling “posts”.",-1),e("p",null,"But there’s a predictable consequence to having this one game take up so many pages: it means suddenly I have to make A LOT OF EXTRA NOTES. And I’m sorry folks, but I just don’t think I can in good conscience come up with unique notes for when Karkat finds a fucking bottle of Faygo in a chest, or a couple boondollars in a chest next to a transporter after walking down five flights of stairs or something. This is as good an excuse as any to begin reinstituting the GENIUS policy I introduced in the previous book, which can be summarized as “not doing notes for certain pages, if I don’t feel like it”. I can just skip those pages! It’s totally fine. Sure, you’ll miss me on those pages, but you know what they say. Being apart makes the heart grow fonder. And when I finally come back to say something stupid, you may feel slightly less inclined to throw the book across the room.",-1),e("p",null,"I’m about to start skipping notes like a madman. But first, here Karkat’s bringing up the demon again. It’s this huge mystery, but come on. Let’s be grownups here. It’s just Bec Noir. We all know it. But the story toys with this mystery through its nonlinear presentation. Here he seems to SUSPECT it’s Jack (without the words being totally clear about that), but he’s not sure since Bec Noir looks so different. He need to do more research to confirm it for himself, but he will. Which means later, he knows the demon is Jack, but the story dexterously weaves around having him spill this info to the reader, using methods like we’re seeing above. When the time is exactly right (Jade: Enter) the information is revealed to us in grand fashion. That revelation is presented in a format that is otherwise commonly known as a “major plot development”. I find that’s a pretty good way to reveal things, because having people just say the things can be a little boring. And it would be a shame to waste even a single precious drop of your boredom until I have you exactly where I want you, which is trapped with me, RIGHT down here in the Laugh Gutter. Anyway, on that note, bye for a while.",-1),e("p",null,"Returning to say: You can go up to that computer and “Finish responding to Kanaya in memo.” So even though we see their talksprites, this isn’t actually an in-person conversation. It’s Current Karkat + Past Kanaya. This initiates part of a memo conversation we read in the previous book (page 404, if you care to go back and review the context. or HS page 2567). Back then, we already caught a glimpse of this moment just after he woke up, through a very narrow sliver of what takes place in this game. It reminds us that his entire critical nap was precipitated by the fact that he passed out when Kanaya cut off Tavros’ legs. When he woke up, he finally decided to close the book on this memo to clean up the loose ends, which he’s doing here. Very tidy, of him, and me.",-1),e("p",null,"A lot of these conversations mainly establish Karkat insisting he thinks these aliens are boring and he’ll want nothing to do with them. The lense of dramatic irony of course frames these proclamations as laughable, and even Sollux finds it laughable, because he is privy to the same sense of dramatic irony we are, by reading the future memos. He brings this up, and Karkat naturally pounces on the opportunity to lambaste Future Karkat, oh yeah also Past Karkat, he’s even worse, and maybe while he’s at it, Current Karkat too, because wow, fuck that guy. Since we learn that Karkat was initially reluctant to troll the humans, it retroactively sets up a punchline to this joke that asks “what made him change his mind?” And we already know the punchline. It’s because he falls in hate with John.",-1),e("p",null,"Gamzee allows you to sleep on the horn pile for 420 boondollars. In this sense, he’s like an RPG innkeeper. Except the service he is offering is worse than useless. Just like him.",-1),e("p",null,"Last panel, translation: I think you should consider scoping out that Egbert kid’s sweet ass before you write them all off as a waste of time.",-1),e("p",null,"Here’s another key point, which I’m not sure if I referenced yet. Karkat’s Big Speech. It’s a moment on the timeline that we’ve seen numerous allusions to already. We have no idea how good the speech is, because we don’t get to read it. Some trolls seem to think it was good, like Kanaya. But one thing is indisputable. It was directly inspired by how hot Karkat thought John was.",-1),e("p",null,"Karkat orders Equius never to give anyone horse legs. Which I believe is an order he manages to follow. His ancestor, Horuss, however is a different story. But I doubt Kankri ever specifically ordered him not to give Rufioh horse legs. Or he did, but Horuss didn’t listen, because people somehow take Kankri even less seriously than Karkat.",-1),e("p",null,"I’m just going to use this conversation as an excuse point out that one reason this game format is really good is it gives practically everyone and excuse to talk to everybody, without making a huge deal out of it, and wildly inflating the online page count. God I’m so clever. All I REALLY managed to do was wildly inflate the book page count, many years later.",-1),e("p",null,"Karkat makes a pretty good point. If he was going to get robo-legs at all, why not during the session which would have allowed him greater mobility in an environment which is so physically demanding? And Tavros makes a pretty disturbing point. He didn’t actually consent to having his legs sawed off and replaced?? These people are disasters. It does make you wonder about Kanaya’s motivation. Mothering gone overboard? Erasing the most major lasting effect of Vriska’s bullying, which she continued to use as ammunition against him for years after, including during the session? Perhaps the answer to this second question also answers the first posed by Karkat. Kanaya and Equius never got around to the leg transplant during the session because they could never find a moment alone with Tavros without Vriska looming over him all the time. They got to this meteor, found their chance, and took it.",-1),e("p",null,"The rest of the conversation is dedicated to making sure we all know that Tavros doesn’t have the slightest idea what’s actually going on right now. Sounds about right.",-1),e("p",null,"Nepeta is understandably one of the most excited trolls about the human discovery. Possibly more excited than anyone. And she was forbidden from talking to them, which caused Karkat to order her to talk to them a lot out of spite for Equius. So given that she’s excited about the humans, and has been COMMANDED by her crush to interact with them, it’s a little surprising that we barely ever see Nepeta talk to the humans on-screen. Or is it? It probably just means she’s had dozens of off-screen conversations with all of them, all throughout the timeline. They just weren’t important enough to show, because it was just a bunch of useless roleplaying, and also Nepeta is a borderline useless character with nothing that important to say about anything. Sorry Nep, but your metatextually exaggerated uselessness as a minor character has been well established as a critical feature of your profile in many of these author notes by now. Haven’t you been paying attention??",-1),e("p",null,"We don’t get a lot of banter from Aradiabot before this. Aside from her vicious beatdown of Vriska, which characterized her pretty well through action alone, we didn’t have much of a sense for what she’s like verbally, in contrast with her ghost self, and formerly alive self. She’s incredibly moody, angry, fatalistic, lets timeline “spoilers” slip casually, but doesn’t care due to her intense nihilism, and also the fact that nothing can really be changed. She is one of the striking early examples of revealing the full depth of a character’s comprehensive personality by showing different facets of herself come out through different “versions” of the character.",-1),e("p",null,"She’s kind of a linear example of that concept though. She goes from alive -> ghost -> frog -> robot -> alive again, all in sequence, in the same timeline. Which means these facets come across through character evolution, not through hypothetical alternate presentations. Distributed characterization approaches happen a lot more later through those methods with splintered alternate selves, such as through Dave - Davesprite - Davepeta, Jade - Jadesprite, Dirk - AR Dirk - Arquiusprite, or Vriska - (Vriska). Aradia serves as a pretty good trial run for some of these ideas, partly by illustrating how different one person can be under different circumstances.",-1),e("p",null,"Another quick hop-to-the-future vignette. Establishes another key point on the timeline to keep track of, which is “Aradiabot Explodes”. Similar to how we had a “Jadebot Explodes” key moment to track, and then it was revealed in Descend, which covered the “when” and “why” about it. So we’re possibly set up to expect a similar type of revelation concerning this moment. Its revelation is nearly on the same energy wavelength of narrative sensationalism as Descend, broadcast through an animation covering the destruction of Prospit and Derse. This is around when A5A2 is going full blast. There’s a significant period in this sub-act when the crazy intense stuff just never seems to let up. But these stockpiled little mysteries have to be seeded like this for those revelations to have the right impact.",-1),e("p",null,"By now we know that Karkat’s “trolling campaign”, which seemed like it was The Whole Point of the trolls back in Act 4, is completely trivial and comically meaningless. And we have a great appreciation for why that is now, because we know two facts: 1. There are several trolls on this roster who are much more ruthless and cunning than he is, mainly revolving around the Scourge Sister dynamic we didn’t know about before. And 2. Karkat spent a long time asleep on the floor before he even heard of the humans, enabling the troublemakers to run wild hatching plans and messing with humans before he had a chance to do anything. So the real “shadow plan” related to the troll engagement with humans was always overwhelmingly focused on Vriska and Terezi’s intense meddling effort. But we had no appreciation for this at all in Act 4, because our gateway to learning about the trolls was all presented through Karkat’s screaming. In a way, his blustering trolling effort was a great decoy for Vriska and Terezi to use as cover, while they went about their scheming a little more under the radar.",-1),e("p",null,"Being able to switch characters is an important part of the “feel” you get from these games. Loses a LITTLE something here (but what doesn’t??) When you assume control of a new character, the song changes, and it alters the perspective a little. Now you go around talking to people as Vriska instead of Karkat, and you have to switch your psychology a bit. Two very different points of view, different agendas. Vriska is going to offer a very different side to these conversations now, and the responses and attitudes she elicits from others is going to be different too. These are pretty obvious points I’m making? But it’s worth noting, because splashing this type of content into the rest of the story really illustrates how it differs as a storytelling tool, what kind of things you can do with it, and the many interesting ways it shapes reader perception compared to the more static content.",-1),e("p",null,"Looks like Kanaya’s still a little frosty from their breakup. You know, for the relationship they had, which never took place anywhere except for in Kanaya’s mind. The breakup also took place in her mind. You can understand why she’s a bit upset.",-1),e("p",null,"Vriska unsurprisingly is quite obtuse about why Kanaya has been standoffish since she gave her the fairy dress. I’m sure I commented on this at the time. But Vriska speculating that Kanaya had a thing for Tavros is just a shout out to the bold individuals who speculated the same thing when she saw them kiss. It’s about as humorously dense for a reader to make that speculation as it is for Vriska to do the same thing here.",-1),e("p",null,"The best explanation for why Gamzee says he’s scared of Vriska, in my opinion, is this: he’s flat out lying. It’s a good way for him to maintain his cover as “Soft Gamzee”. It also provides some ammunition for those who, against all sense of good taste and judgment, want to continue to believe and assert that Gamzee was a decent guy with sensitive emotions and vulnerabilities before he underwent his murderstuck awakening. He was none of those things, ever.",-1),e("p",null,"Her brief conversations with Kanaya and Eridan in close vicinity help convey a likely truth about Vriska. Most of the “relationships” she has tend to take place inside the minds of other people she messes with. The only one for whom this definitively proves not to be true in the long run is Terezi.",-1),e("p",null,"Another future hop, another explosion to track. Put another pin on the insane timeline you’ve got scattered all over your wall like a lunatic, and continue. All of this will be explained. This incident involves Rose, in response to conduct from Eridan which will in no way come as surprising.",-1),e("p",null,"Actually... it’s basically explained here. What I mean is, we will get to read this conversation in due time. Also, no, Vriska does not detect blackrom in the air. Rose isn’t interested either. No one is, or ever will be. Wait, I take that back. Cronus is the only exception.",-1),e("p",null,"One of the many great things about Vriska is she just genuinely does not give the slightest fuck about so many things. The “cutting through the bullshit” part of her personality is mainly what’s behind her power as a narrative utility which can turbocharge the plot and move things along in compelling albeit chaotic ways. But another advantage to this quality is it allows me to bring certain conversational trains of thought to a dead stop sometimes. We really don’t need to read more of this shit right now, Equius. Sorry man.",-1),e("p",null,"Just an incredible line, in that second panel. Sometimes I have to sit back and just marvel at the great things I made her say. Or was she the one who made me make her say the things? I wouldn’t be surprised if that’s true. I think the next self-insert segment raises this possibility implicitly, by putting her little icon over my forehead. What’s the truth? Is she the one writing all this?? I think we start to see, as her plan with human meddling starts to play out, that this is exactly what she’s trying to do, at least in a retroactive sense. Overwriting the actions of others with her own intent, putting her “brand” on things, casting herself as “responsible” in hindsight, for actions which she technically did not author. Though it seems like a cheap trick, it really does have the effect of blurring original intent, and we can lose our bearings on who exactly was responsible for what. So I’ll ask again. Who’s more real, Vriska or me? Not so sure now, are you???",-1),e("p",null,"Nepeta, how fucking DARE you stifle Vriska’s creativity. Her spidersona is probably GREAT. You see? Shit like this is why you get such a raw deal in this story. Maybe if you were cooler and played ball with her spidersona and Mindfang a little more, things could have turned out differently for you. It didn’t have to be this way... just sad.",-1),e("p",null,"This conversation provides a very rare glimpse into the possibility that Feferi may have a more interesting personality than she gets credit for. Playing mind games to own Vriska, even if it’s just in a meaningless throwaway conversation, isn’t a bad thing to have on your resume. But again, it’s my sad duty to remind you that for whatever other surprises may exist in the full range of her profile, she just doesn’t last much longer than this. She and Nepeta really can’t catch a break. Luckily they both experience profound narrative redemption much later, in the form of Fefeta. I believe Fefeta’s stirring ten page soliloquy after she gets created really captures the pathos behind their mutual struggle with narrative irrelevance. Keep an eye out for that.",-1),e("p",null,"Sure, Aradiabot could probably break her neck. But what we aren’t supposed to know yet is Vriska’s god tier. So would it be a Just death? I guess it depends on how sick the burn was that warranted having her neck snapped by Aradia. Maybe that’s why Aradia doesn’t bother? These burns are kind of weak, so she knows Vriska would just come back to life.",-1),e("p",null,"Sollux x Feferi is a romance which in my view is quite deftly handled off-screen. You see hints of it, like how Feferi’s fishspeak slips into Sollux’s language. And people notice, like Vriska, and get annoyed. Which is a thing that’s been going on in a larger sense, actually. Characters detecting traces of other characters’ speech patterns rubbing off on each other, and using this as a clue that something romantic is brewing. Vriska with John, Terezi with Dave. Even Jade starts flagrantly swearing more than she typically does after more interaction with Karkat. Rose and Kanaya already basically talk like each other, so they can’t really tip other people off to what’s going on there. Although I guess you could infer that the moment Rose Starts Talking Like This, maybe it’s a sign that they both finally went all the way. Not that this ever actually happens. Although to be fair, by the time they’re both in a position for anything like that to hypothetically take place, Rose is drunk off her ass anyway.",-1),e("p",null,"You can transportalize to another room full of transporters, each one labeled with a troll sign. Only the troll of that sign can use it, as if each one took the time to specifically claim one and lock everyone else out. When did they find the time to do this?? It seems very organized of them, to secure their on private spaces on the meteor. Especially considering their hasty getaway resulting from a crisis. Maybe trolls have such a strong instinctive sense of private space, groups of trolls always do this any time they find a new space to commonly settle into? It would make sense, since they all grow up solo in their own very personalized hive. Yeah, let’s say that’s the explanation for this ridiculous feature of this game map.",-1),e("p",null,"Ok, there’s a lot to keep up with on this page. I know this comic gets pretty dense sometimes, but hang in there. If you really take the time to study it, you’ll get it all eventually. Keep an eye out for the Author Notes Expansion Pack later, wherein I expound upon this page with the sense of thoroughness it deserves.",-1),e("p",null,"She keeps the severed Tavros legs in a chest because... she just does. She’s Vriska. She gets to do stuff like that, and we don’t get to ask why. Any questions? You aren’t allowed to have questions. Here’s a tangential consideration which you may not want to think about it, but let’s just consider this your punishment for daring to question Vriska’s motives. If you take into account Tavros’ lower torso which has been hoarded here, and the existence of alchemy, I think we can begin to see the basic constituents for the origin of the bull penis cane. An item which, like many things on this meteor, eventually finds its way to the Felt Mansion in the future. Now, did you NEED to be forced to think about how Vriska may have quite literally used the mutilated remains of her ex boyfriend to make a bovine dildo? No, you didn’t. But as I said, you brought this upon yourself.",-1),e("p",null,"We’ve already spent a little time on a similar meteor in the veil in the human session. So we know stuff like this is around. Big glass tubes full of test subjects, which usually contain big mutant chess freaks that get deployed on the Battlefield. Here’s a big one that’s busted. We’re gettin foreshadowed at here, in a way that games commonly do. Uh oh! There should be a monster safely contained here, and yet, it is not here. Seems to have escaped! Suuuure hope you do not have to confront this beast in some way later!",-1),e("p",null,"Here’s this boss battle. It’s not real of course. There’s no combat or anything. You just choose some options and it sort of plays itself. Who even has time to “make real games”, when the games in question take several days to make? This was a pretty fun diversion though. We even switched sprite style briefly, from what is usually an Earthbound-inspired look, to what I believe was mimicking the sprite style in a Scott Pilgrim game. If this were a real battle, it would probably be pretty strenuous. I’m guessing his udder would be some sort of weak point?? But Vriska just cheats her way through it, which is fine, because we don’t have all day.",-1),e("p",null,"She’s the Thief of Light. So she literally steals his luck, and gives it to herself. This makes it much more likely something bad will happen to him, all on its own.",-1),e("p",null,"Like this. He falls in a pit, and that’s the end of this guy forever. Take a note of this pit she just created though. Remember the context, and the shape of those jagged edges. This precipitous venue comes back into play during a critical showdown with Tavros, wherein dramatic happenings take place. (FYI - she just throws the dude off another fuckin cliff.)",-1),e("p",null,"The items are a bit ridiculous. Finding them in chests serves no purpose at all, except to sort of amusingly emulate the feel of playing an RPG. Otherwise there’s very little logic to it. Why WOULDN’T she keep he dice on her all the time? Especially since a sylladex allows you to do this very easily, without feeling encumbered. The answer is, they’re all completely ridiculous people, who like to hoard things in their secret locked-away parts of the meteor, especially the ones who have a flair for treasure and the hunting thereof. Bear in mind she originally found her cool dice in a chest on a ship once. Maybe she just likes to relive the magic of that experience now and then??",-1),e("p",null,"Speaking of treasure, here’s her secret pirate’s trove. Which, yes, due to inflation is now completely worthless. But it was already worthless, since she was never going to use it to buy anything ever again anyway. Let’s try to imagine how many times more money Terezi has than this now. If this is the size of Vriska’s stockpile, then Terezi’s would be... the size of the sun? Bigger?? Probably bigger. No wonder Vriska’s steamed. Time to spend the rest of the act radically overcompensating.",-1),e("p",null,"W... wow?",-1),e("p",null,"More Strong Content.",-1),e("p",null,"In the future I guess she cleaned out her whole supply of boondollars. I forget what happened there... possibly gave it all to John to help him compete with Dave? That sounds right, even if it’s not. Let’s just say it is. I believe this flash-forward triggered some poignant music, because she’s about to express her undying love for Nic Cage again.",-1),e("p",null,"She stashed a dreamy Cage bust away in here, understandably. And also... a salamander? Ok. I guess she... kidnapped one? Again, let’s remind ourselves we aren’t allowed to question anything Vriska does. It’s totally fine she trapped a helpless salamander in this chest for many hours.",-1),e("p",null,"Her Cage Shrine is where she goes to be alone with her most private thoughts, and to put in some good swooning time over her main boy Nic. Examining this wall triggers this... I’m not sure what it is. Some half-assed Flash garbage, which jitters about. Nic Cage, in terms of the mythological language and symbolism native to Homestuck, conforms to the archetype of “Human Shitpost”.",-1),e("p",null,"We’re back. That was the completion of a minor “arc”, on this minigame’s terms. You get to the Cage room, and that’s that for Vriska. It unlocks another part of the game you can play, which starts here.",-1),e("p",null,"She’s frustrated beyond words that her bonehead ex almost-boyfriend doesn’t see what the big deal is about a moon made of gold. Just another indication that what she really values is a partner who has a deep appreciation for the value of riches. Dave beats him in that regard easily. But it also sheds a little more light on the fact that no one will ever truly be able to compete with Vriska when it comes to Terezi’s fixation.",-1),e("p",null,"You have to be pretty alert to tell when someone is hitting on you in the clubs quadrant. It’s subtle enough to miss it. “Hey, could you tell that bastard over there he’s a bastard for me? And don’t you DARE think of watering down my message of hate for me, so as to moderate this unpleasant rivalry I’m placing you in the middle of!” Ok, that’s an obvious clubs solicitation. But usually they don’t sound like that. You know how sometimes in bar fights, there’s the guy who wants to start a fight, but he tries to get his friends to “hold him back”? That’s textbook solicitation in the ashen quadrant. Apparently rowdy, drunken bros do it all the time.",-1),e("p",null,"Take note of the phrase “unexpected honk jump” as a sign of oblique, yet retroactively blatant foreshadowing for the Gamrezi ship.",-1),e("p",null,"Finally we can ferret out some new plot-relevant information from an obscure pocket of this game. We last saw Rose talking about the Green Sun upon revealing its existence, but heard nothing of a plan involving using it in some way to defeat Jack Noir. This is some advance notice to watch a plan like that to start coming together. It’s just a little tidbit to get us thinking. Still lots of missing information, including: Jack is also the demon the trolls are hiding from, he’s been Bec-ified, thus he draws his energy from the Green Sun, ergo it stands to reason that destroying the sun will weaken him. We have quite a ways to go to get up to speed on all that, unless you don’t mind skipping down here to my notes where I casually and carelessly spoil you on literally everything, because I don’t really see a good reason not to.",-1),e("p",null,"A little glimpse into the background of how their boyfight proxy-rivalry kicked off. Terezi seems like she got dragged into it a little, seems reluctant, considers a lot of Vriska’s goading childish, crazy and obsessive, but at the end of the day, Terezi seems to get just as into the boy rivalry as Vriska does. This is probably how a lot of their competitions went in the past. It also speaks to this addictive quality that this unhealthy aspect of their relationship has, particularly for Terezi. She knows it’s not good for her, but can’t seem to help getting roped back into the bullshit. After Vriska’s death, Terezi’s guilt-slash-withdrawal symptoms lead her to fill the addiction void with a trash clown. Which is a bit like being on some potent controlled substance, getting cut off, and getting hooked on the hard stuff to compensate. Shooting up in back alleys, turning tricks in exchange for more... actually in Gamrezi’s case, those two ideas end up being about the same thing.",-1),e("p",null,"Fair question: who’s a worse influence on Tavros? Vriska or Terezi? Sure, Vriska paralyzed him in the first place, and pretty soon she will murder him in cold blood. But at least she keeps it real. For some reason Terezi insists on enabling his bullshit with a lot of misguided positivity and support. Just sickening, really. In due time, Terezi will pay a very steep karmic price for this crime, by having her Vriska privileges revoked.",-1),e("p",null,"Then Tavros fucks off, to go fall down a whole bunch of stairs. We’ll catch up with his point of view later. Mainly by watching him fall down some stairs.",-1),e("p",null,"Nepeta almost immediately confirms with Terezi that she’s been roleplaying with Dave somewhere on the timeline, by referencing his fursona, Akwete Purrmusk. This seems like a dumb throwaway gag that will be meaningless forever, and it very nearly was. Until at the last minute, the story dumped Davepeta in our laps. She literally fuses with another version of this guy to help him fully realize his fursona. A truly heartwarming endpoint for both their arcs.",-1),e("p",null,"Nepeta, maybe Jade just isn’t into it? Maybe learn to take a fucking hint?? Sure, it seems like you two SHOULD get along, but what if that’s just not how things always work? Don’t be a rando. Just chill out. Mr. Purrmusk has shown a perfectly reliable willingness to tolerate your nonsense. Strider’s roleplay fields have proven to be rich and fertile. Now stop wasting Jade’s time, and go embrace your destiny.",-1),e("p",null,"Feferi is right, the humans are the answer. But not because they actually have a good plan for solving all the problems. That never happens, in fact. The real reason is because they’re the main characters. Leave it to one of the least important characters to have sort of a sixth sense when it comes to identifying the most important characters, when no one else can.",-1),e("p",null,"I said that we’d catch up with Tavros and his struggle with stairs later, but I forgot that actually happens within the same game. So what this means is, there are even more moments of Tavros falling down the stairs to look forward to after this game is finished. Pretty exciting stuff.",-1),e("p",null,"The meteor has little puzzles to solve. Don’t ask why. It’s a game. Not just one you’re playing. They’re playing it too. At some point Karkat actually did have to journey through this meteor as a Sgrub objective to create all their baby grubs in the first place, through ectobiology. So yeah, he probably had to do menial things like pushing clone jars on to special tiles to unlock doors, while hating every minute of it.",-1),e("p",null,"That was a lot of consecutive pages without notes, involving a mostly uneventful transit through Terezi’s private section of the map. Maybe this makes it seem like this game’s map involves a gratuitous sprawl which mostly serves as a gameplay time sink, to flesh out the environmental color. But I think more is being established here. In a fairly efficient way, giving us a good sense of “space” and “setting” tied to this meteor location, which will become one of the most significantly revisited places in the story. Here’s a location in the meteor being established now, a place where Terezi does chalk drawings on the wall. Later she adds to these drawings after befriending Dave. And much later, when Dave himself is on this meteor, they start doing drawings on the walls together, while the Mayor works on Can Town. Many locations you pass through in this game are revisited later as little location callbacks, and if you’re on the ball, you remember them when you see them.",-1),e("p",null,"Here’s how these timeline anchor points help us get our bearings. She smells the remains of Prospit, which recently blew up. So in a couple pages when we time-skip ahead to smelling the remains of Derse, we have a pretty good sense of where we are in the story now.",-1),e("p",null,"The empty chest which you may fill in the future is just a time-hop transition trick, to bring us to...",-1),e("p",null,"The death of Derse, pinning us down about three hours in the future. A lot has happened in those three hours. Now through the power of this flash-forward, we get to backtrack through Terezi’s lair, and get a sense of what the developments have been.",-1),e("p",null,"The empty chest transition device is also being used for a little wardrobe change, to help visually distinguish between the Terezi from this time period, and the Terezi from the past we just got done controlling. Now if we ever see Dragon Suit Terezi in future panels, it can be used as another bit of evidence regarding where to put her on the timeline.",-1),e("p",null,"Three hours later, the full blown Davening of Terezi is well underway. My feeling has always been, there are just certain types of people with specific personalities who were born to be diehard fans of SBaHJ. Terezi is clearly one of them.",-1),e("p",null,"Nepeta’s hangin out in the radial transporter array room for some reason. She’s here to show you a cool secret. It’s very exciting.",-1),e("p",null,"Kind of a standard RPG secret format. You look for vulnerabilities in the wall, and sometimes certain spots are intangible, which lead you to hidden rooms. But I don’t think you were allowed to find this secret until Nepeta led the way, if I recall.",-1),e("p",null,"Earlier, Eridan made some reference to being more discreet with his pile of wands than Gamzee was with his pile of horns. Like it’s shameful contraband which a guy should keep tucked away, rather than letting it all hang out in the open like a shameless clown. So Nepeta’s helped us stumble on Eridan’s stash. Is nothing sacred???",-1),e("p",null,"Back to past Terezi before entering the computer lab again. Walking around and talking to people in the lab in the three-hours future would expose too much info for now. We got enough of a taste.",-1),e("p",null,"Karkat’s turn. Again it should be reminded, that you can do a lot of this in any order you want. This is just the way it’s been presented here. Karkat seemed like the best one to save for last. Ultimately, with this format, it’s totally up to the player to decide when to move on and click the link to the next page. They can glean as much or as little from the game as they want, with no particular milestones to clear before allowing the reader to advance. Perhaps this is a double edged sword, storytelling-wise. But there aren’t many other ways to handle it in such a mixed media environment.",-1),e("p",null,"Recall that the Sassacre tome was touted as being thick enough to kill someone (or, a cat specifically), which foreshadowed the fact that it did literally kill someone (nanna), and also literally did kill a cat specifically as well. We’re getting a similar line here. It isn’t QUITE used as a blunt instrument to kill someone exactly. But Doc Scratch does end up using it to put a pretty good beatdown on Spades Slick.",-1),e("p",null,"Finally he enters the same ectobiology lab he used to make paradox clones of himself and all his friends, just like John did. Also just like John, note that there appears to have been twice as many infants created as might be expected. We know that John created their guardians as well, who are all true paradox clones (genetic copies of themselves, and themselves alone paradoxically, and therefore not related to each other). And then all their slime was mixed to create the kids, who are sibling pairs (Jake + Jane = John and Jade. Dirk + Roxy = Dave and Rose.) So this leads us to expect something similar happened here, especially now that we see two identical sets of 12 jars. Except due to the way trolls breed, they don’t have parent-pairs, so the slime wasn’t mixed that way. They arise thought an “incestuous slurry”, which implies all the slime mixed together, and resulted in each troll individually. It seems this process happened twice, once for the trolls we know, and then again for all their ancestors, who seem to be, if not exact copies, extremely close genetic copies of their descendants.",-1),e("p",null,"We flash back to the moment he activated the cloning machine, and see him swarmed by freshly spawned grubs. Now the question is, are these his baby friends, or their ancestors? Which spawned first? It probably doesn’t matter, since they’re so similar. Whichever it was, the other group came next, but that’s not shown here, because we’re not diving into the ancestor stuff just yet at this point of the story. But that second array of 12 jars certainly starts putting the idea in our heads. It’s probably the first concrete evidence that the ancestors are going to be a real thing in the story, rather than something vaguely alluded to as background lore during the Hivebent wordlbuilding gauntlet. If you’re perceptive, you stick this observation in your back pocket, and wait for the issue to come up again. And it does. Later in A5A2, when the dramatic arcs of all these trolls as a friend group start coming to a head, the introduction of all the ancestors as characters (see: Mindfang Diaries) mainly serves as a way of broadening those arcs to function as conclusions to these sort of long-running karmic destinies originally established by their legendary progenitors. Those backstories do a lot more than that too, including expanding on Alternian lore a little more, as well as establishing yet another roster of intensely beloved fan favorites with a few quick story strokes. Quick in page count, albeit kinda wordy (thanks Aranea).",-1),e("p",null,"Aaaaaand that’s the last page of that game. On this adorable note of ancestor-descendant ambiguity, let’s move on. At the end of the day, it was only 150 pages or so, right? It’s not like anybody else was gonna do something better with that paper.",-1)])]),_:1})],64))}});export{T as default};
